    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42489",
    ":question/problem": "Consider the matrices $A$ and $C$ given below. Find $det({A}^{T})-det({C}^{T}).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2)=28 \\\\ det(C) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-3)=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det({A}^{T}) & =det(A)=28 \\\\ det({C}^{T}) & =det(C)=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{T})-det({C}^{T})=28-27=1.\n$$"
  },
