    ":question/id": "ac56422a-ae07-5014-84d3-374ac97d57b5",
    ":question/problem": "Consider the matrices $A$ and $B$ below. Find $\\det(A)-\\det(B^T)$.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0.5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The matrix $A$ is lower triangular and $B$ is upper triangular. Multiply their main diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A)&=(-2)\\cdot3\\cdot1=-6,\\\\\\det(B)&=1\\cdot(-2)\\cdot2=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTransposition preserves the determinant, so $\\det(B^T)=\\det(B)=-4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\det(A)-\\det(B^T)=-6-(-4)=-2.\n$$"
  },
