    ":question/id": "e943bc0b-58ab-5d24-9b76-ad39a0da742b",
    ":question/problem": "Consider the matrices $A$ and $B$ below. Find $\\det(A^T+B)$.\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Transposing the lower triangular matrix $A$ gives an upper triangular matrix. Since $B$ is also upper triangular, their sum is upper triangular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A^T+B&=\\begin{bmatrix}2+(-3) & -2+5 & 4+0 \\\\ 0 & -1+4 & 1+(-2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3+(-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts main diagonal entries are $-1$, $3$, and $2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\det(A^T+B)=(-1)\\cdot3\\cdot2=-6.\n$$\n\nWe must add the matrices before taking the diagonal product. Adding their determinants would give $-6+12=6$, which is a different quantity."
  }
